1
00:00:02,961 --> 00:00:07,716
So... couldn't help
but notice none of you RSVP'd

2
00:00:07,883 --> 00:00:10,093
to my murder mystery dinner party.

3
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:12,804
Oh, yeah.
We were meaning to do that.

4
00:00:12,971 --> 00:00:16,183
No, you weren't, because it
was a week ago and nobody came!

5
00:00:17,559 --> 00:00:19,895
So if you want to solve the mystery
of who stabbed Koothrappali

6
00:00:20,062 --> 00:00:23,357
in the back with the weapon of
indifference, it was all of you.

7
00:00:24,441 --> 00:00:26,068
I don't think that qualifies
as a mystery.

8
00:00:26,235 --> 00:00:27,778
We all knew what we were doing.

9
00:00:29,863 --> 00:00:32,866
-We're sorry, Rajesh.
-Maybe we can do it next week.

10
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:34,910
(LAUGHS)
Whoa, we're not that sorry.

11
00:00:35,077 --> 00:00:38,997
Don't worry, I can take a hint.
No more murder mystery parties.

12
00:00:39,164 --> 00:00:40,457
-Thank you.
-Hallelujah.

13
00:00:40,624 --> 00:00:42,376
Because I've got something
even better planned!

14
00:00:42,543 --> 00:00:45,379
-Come on!
-Just hear me out.

15
00:00:45,546 --> 00:00:47,965
I'm going to make you guys
a scavenger hunt

16
00:00:48,131 --> 00:00:49,424
like they have at MIT.

17
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:53,303
Oh, I loved those.
I did them every year there.

18
00:00:53,470 --> 00:00:55,514
-We did them at Princeton, too.
-Oh, that's cute.

19
00:00:55,681 --> 00:00:56,974
Like it's a real college.

20
00:00:58,517 --> 00:01:00,936
That's amusing. I was going
to say that about MIT,

21
00:01:01,103 --> 00:01:02,813
but it works for Princeton, too.

22
00:01:04,273 --> 00:01:06,275
Scavenger hunts at Harvard
were really tough.

23
00:01:06,441 --> 00:01:08,277
I always got stuck
on the first challenge.

24
00:01:08,443 --> 00:01:10,320
Trying to find someone
to be on a team with me.

25
00:01:10,487 --> 00:01:11,530
(LAUGHS)

26
00:01:12,447 --> 00:01:15,200
I guess that story's
more sad than funny.

27
00:01:17,578 --> 00:01:19,997
I love scavenger hunts. My friends
and I had them all the time.

28
00:01:20,163 --> 00:01:21,206
Oh, these are a little different.

29
00:01:21,373 --> 00:01:23,500
There's a tradition
at elite universities.

30
00:01:23,667 --> 00:01:25,043
And Princeton.

31
00:01:26,378 --> 00:01:30,048
A tradition of hunts full of puzzles
that rely on logic,

32
00:01:30,215 --> 00:01:31,925
obscure knowledge, science.

33
00:01:32,091 --> 00:01:34,595
Oh, in ours we would just run
around town looking for a store

34
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:36,680
that would sell us beer.

35
00:01:38,097 --> 00:01:40,601
-So, who's in?
-I think it sounds fun.

36
00:01:40,767 --> 00:01:41,768
-Yeah, me, too.
-I'm in.

37
00:01:41,935 --> 00:01:43,937
-Let's do it.
-I'm sorry.

38
00:01:44,103 --> 00:01:46,732
Are we really doing this
or are we tricking Koothrappali again

39
00:01:46,898 --> 00:01:48,525
like with the dinner party?

40
00:01:50,235 --> 00:01:52,654
(THEME SONG PLAYING)

41
00:02:14,134 --> 00:02:15,344
What's all this?

42
00:02:15,510 --> 00:02:18,305
Oh, everything I could possibly need
to win the scavenger hunt.

43
00:02:18,472 --> 00:02:19,806
The Scavenger Vortex

44
00:02:19,973 --> 00:02:21,725
You really think he's gonna
send us to a bowling alley?

45
00:02:23,018 --> 00:02:26,063
Well, if he does, do you know how
filthy those rental balls are?

46
00:02:27,564 --> 00:02:28,815
They might as well
stand on the corner

47
00:02:28,982 --> 00:02:30,817
and give away free rectal exams.

48
00:02:33,028 --> 00:02:34,780
(AMPLIFIED BANGING)
Ahem.

49
00:02:34,946 --> 00:02:36,907
Okay, everybody.

50
00:02:37,074 --> 00:02:38,575
Who's ready for a...

51
00:02:39,368 --> 00:02:39,951
Oh!

52
00:02:40,118 --> 00:02:43,664
...scavenger hunt?
(ECHOING)

53
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:49,336
(ELECTRONIC DANCE
MUSIC PLAYING)

54
00:02:58,220 --> 00:02:59,346
(MUSIC STOPS)

55
00:03:00,722 --> 00:03:03,767
Somewhere... in the city of Pasadena,

56
00:03:03,934 --> 00:03:08,187
I've hidden a golden coin! (ECHOING)

57
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:14,528
You will be faced with a total
of ten puzzles! (ECHOING)

58
00:03:18,031 --> 00:03:19,574
Each-- (COUGHS)

59
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:24,162
Each... each puzzle will lead you

60
00:03:24,329 --> 00:03:26,205
to the location of the next,

61
00:03:26,373 --> 00:03:30,460
the last of which
will lead you to the coin. (ECHOING)

62
00:03:32,504 --> 00:03:37,050
The first team that finds it... wins!

63
00:03:38,969 --> 00:03:40,595
He is a born showman.

64
00:03:42,806 --> 00:03:45,058
Any questions? (ECHOING)

65
00:03:46,476 --> 00:03:50,397
Yes, to be fair, do people who went
to Princeton get a head start?

66
00:03:50,564 --> 00:03:51,940
That's not funny.

67
00:03:52,107 --> 00:03:55,026
Oh, it actually is,
if you get the joke.

68
00:03:55,193 --> 00:03:58,947
It's based on the premise that
Princeton isn't a very good school.

69
00:03:59,114 --> 00:04:00,115
Ha-ha.

70
00:04:00,323 --> 00:04:02,534
Oh, see? Now, he gets it.

71
00:04:03,869 --> 00:04:06,788
Okay, guys, go ahead and divide
yourselves into teams of two.

72
00:04:06,955 --> 00:04:09,666
-Should we just do couples?
-Couples sounds great.

73
00:04:09,833 --> 00:04:14,004
Or we could mix things up.
Maybe pick names out of a hat, whatever.

74
00:04:14,171 --> 00:04:17,257
Whoa, whoa, whoa.

75
00:04:17,424 --> 00:04:20,218
Why don't you just admit you don't
want to be on a team with me.

76
00:04:20,385 --> 00:04:22,262
I just said, "Couples sound great."

77
00:04:22,429 --> 00:04:24,222
Mm-hmm, yeah, you don't
think I'm smart enough.

78
00:04:24,389 --> 00:04:26,099
You think I'm gonna be a liability.

79
00:04:26,265 --> 00:04:28,894
Even though I totally just used
the word "liability" correctly

80
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:30,645
in a sentence.

81
00:04:32,189 --> 00:04:34,024
Yeah.

82
00:04:34,191 --> 00:04:36,485
No, let's do couples. I want to.

83
00:04:36,651 --> 00:04:39,029
No, no, no. Let's mix things up.

84
00:04:39,196 --> 00:04:41,114
I choose Sheldon,
and we're gonna kick your ass!

85
00:04:41,281 --> 00:04:43,492
Yeah-- really?

86
00:04:43,658 --> 00:04:45,284
The only time I'm ever
picked first for a team

87
00:04:45,452 --> 00:04:47,496
and I'm stuck with the liability?

88
00:04:49,331 --> 00:04:51,333
Stop that.
Penny is not a liability.

89
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:53,251
Thank you.
Do you want to be on my team?

90
00:04:53,418 --> 00:04:55,545
Maybe we pick names out of a hat.

91
00:04:58,673 --> 00:05:00,383
Hurry up and close the door
so they can't hear us.

92
00:05:05,722 --> 00:05:09,726
Would you stop pouting?
So, you picked my name. Get over it.

93
00:05:09,893 --> 00:05:14,105
Yes, and do you know what
the odds are I'd pick your name?

94
00:05:14,272 --> 00:05:17,359
-No.
-It's not hard: one in five.

95
00:05:17,526 --> 00:05:19,318
Now you know why I'm pouting.

96
00:05:21,279 --> 00:05:24,533
RAJ (OVER RADIO): All right, teams.
Get ready to open your first puzzle.

97
00:05:24,699 --> 00:05:26,201
Go!

98
00:05:28,745 --> 00:05:32,374
Do you see what I did?
The first puzzle is a puzzle!

99
00:05:32,541 --> 00:05:36,127
Oh, my God, how adorable is that?

100
00:05:36,294 --> 00:05:38,588
I wish I had a friend like me.

101
00:05:39,673 --> 00:05:42,341
What are you doing?
You have to start with the edges.

102
00:05:42,551 --> 00:05:45,178
There's no right way, Sheldon.
I already found a few pieces that fit.

103
00:05:45,344 --> 00:05:48,598
Yeah, well, take them apart
and start with the edges.

104
00:05:50,183 --> 00:05:51,810
And stop wasting time.

105
00:05:53,812 --> 00:05:56,022
Wow, you're really good at puzzles.

106
00:05:56,189 --> 00:05:57,356
I did them all the time as a kid.

107
00:05:57,524 --> 00:05:59,943
As my mom used to say,
"When you're doing a puzzle,

108
00:06:00,110 --> 00:06:02,612
it's like having a thousand friends."

109
00:06:04,906 --> 00:06:07,200
She was full
of fun lies like that.

110
00:06:07,366 --> 00:06:11,162
If it makes you feel any better,
my mom's just full of pound cake.

111
00:06:12,706 --> 00:06:13,915
I'm sorry you got stuck with me.

112
00:06:14,082 --> 00:06:15,417
I bet you wanted
to be with Bernadette.

113
00:06:16,668 --> 00:06:18,086
Have you ever played
a game with Bernadette?

114
00:06:18,253 --> 00:06:19,838
-No.
-Have you ever gone

115
00:06:20,005 --> 00:06:22,465
into a steel cage with a wolverine?

116
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:26,052
(YELLING) Faster, faster, faster!
Do you not know that word?

117
00:06:26,219 --> 00:06:29,931
-It means "more fast"!
-Stop yelling at me.

118
00:06:30,098 --> 00:06:33,852
Hey! You'll know
when I'm yelling at you.

119
00:06:34,436 --> 00:06:36,688
Oh. Oh, it's the comic book store!
All right, come on.

120
00:06:36,855 --> 00:06:38,148
That's where we have to go.

121
00:06:38,315 --> 00:06:39,733
But we haven't finished the puzzle.

122
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:43,236
Well, it doesn't matter!
We know the answer!

123
00:06:43,402 --> 00:06:47,115
You think you know the answer.
But it could be a trick.

124
00:06:47,282 --> 00:06:49,075
What if when the puzzle's complete,

125
00:06:49,242 --> 00:06:50,994
there's a sign
in the window that says,

126
00:06:51,161 --> 00:06:52,662
"Go to the train store"?

127
00:06:52,829 --> 00:06:54,664
Okay, it's not gonna say that.

128
00:06:54,831 --> 00:06:58,960
Well, I hope you're wrong.
I really want to go to the train store.

129
00:07:00,754 --> 00:07:03,381
It's the comic book store. Let's go!

130
00:07:03,548 --> 00:07:06,635
Come on, numb nuts,
it's the comic book store!

131
00:07:12,891 --> 00:07:14,935
It's the comic book store,
it's the comic book store,

132
00:07:15,101 --> 00:07:16,603
it's the comic book store!

133
00:07:19,438 --> 00:07:20,690
(GROANS)

134
00:07:27,238 --> 00:07:29,074
-It's the comic book store.
-Oh!

135
00:07:35,413 --> 00:07:37,958
-Do you think Penny's mad at me?
-Because you're in the right lane

136
00:07:38,124 --> 00:07:42,212
behind a bus and won't go around it?
I'm sure she finds it charming.

137
00:07:42,379 --> 00:07:45,173
No, because I didn't
want her on my team.

138
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:47,634
I'm starting to think
she dodged a bullet.

139
00:07:47,801 --> 00:07:49,761
The slowest bullet in the world.

140
00:07:51,596 --> 00:07:53,807
I never realized
you were so competitive.

141
00:07:53,974 --> 00:07:56,017
Yeah, I know.

142
00:07:56,184 --> 00:07:58,895
It's probably because I grew up
with five brothers and sisters

143
00:07:59,062 --> 00:08:00,063
so I had to fight for every--

144
00:08:00,230 --> 00:08:02,607
Oh, my God, you did not
just slow down for a bird!

145
00:08:02,774 --> 00:08:04,567
You know they fly, right?!

146
00:08:10,365 --> 00:08:11,574
I think this is
the first time we've ever

147
00:08:11,741 --> 00:08:13,451
actually been alone together.

148
00:08:13,618 --> 00:08:17,247
Huh, I guess you're right.
Wonder why that is.

149
00:08:17,414 --> 00:08:20,250
Well, off the top of my head,
I'd say we don't have a lot

150
00:08:20,417 --> 00:08:22,877
in common except for the people
we're involved with.

151
00:08:23,044 --> 00:08:25,088
Plus, when we first met,
Penny warned me never

152
00:08:25,255 --> 00:08:27,590
to get into a car alone with you.

153
00:08:28,967 --> 00:08:30,552
Yeah.

154
00:08:32,178 --> 00:08:35,765
You know, I bet you and I have
more in common than we think.

155
00:08:35,932 --> 00:08:38,601
-Such as?
-I don't know.

156
00:08:38,768 --> 00:08:41,730
-Tell me some things you like.
-Uh, let's see.

157
00:08:41,896 --> 00:08:45,066
I like harps,
Little House on the Prairie,

158
00:08:45,233 --> 00:08:47,235
medieval poetry,
medieval literature--

159
00:08:47,402 --> 00:08:49,195
Hey, how about some music?

160
00:08:50,655 --> 00:08:53,408
Great. Sheldon never lets me
listen to music in the car.

161
00:08:53,575 --> 00:08:56,077
He doesn't want to be mistaken
for a gang member.

162
00:08:56,244 --> 00:08:58,955
Find something you like.

163
00:08:59,122 --> 00:09:01,458
Beatles, boring.

164
00:09:01,624 --> 00:09:03,918
Eminem, scary.

165
00:09:04,085 --> 00:09:06,504
Weird Al? How old are you?

166
00:09:07,714 --> 00:09:11,885
-Neil Diamond?
-Yeah, I love Neil Diamond.

167
00:09:12,052 --> 00:09:13,678
I love Neil Diamond.

168
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:21,269
(SINGING NEIL DIAMOND'S
"SWEET CAROLINE")

169
00:09:31,613 --> 00:09:33,823
Oh, hey, thanks for letting me
use the comic book store

170
00:09:33,990 --> 00:09:35,909
-as part of the scavenger hunt.
-Oh, my pleasure.

171
00:09:36,076 --> 00:09:37,869
Always happy to help out
with fun things

172
00:09:38,036 --> 00:09:40,455
that I wasn't invited
to be a part of.

173
00:09:40,622 --> 00:09:44,501
Oh, congratulations.
You're the first team to arrive.

174
00:09:44,667 --> 00:09:47,253
Yes! It's not a ruse
to make fun of me!

175
00:09:47,420 --> 00:09:50,006
It's a real game and I'm winning it!

176
00:09:51,674 --> 00:09:54,886
Your next puzzle is a riddle.

177
00:09:55,053 --> 00:09:59,015
And who better to give it to you
than The Riddler?

178
00:10:00,433 --> 00:10:04,395
Go, go, go, go, go, go, go, go,
go, go, go, go, go, go, go!

179
00:10:04,562 --> 00:10:06,189
How am I faster than you?

180
00:10:06,356 --> 00:10:09,442
I'm in heels and I stopped
to take a phone call.

181
00:10:09,608 --> 00:10:13,905
I have asthma. Back off!

182
00:10:14,072 --> 00:10:18,326
"Riddle me this.
Arrah, Arrah, and gather round,

183
00:10:18,493 --> 00:10:21,204
the work of this hero
is legion-bound.

184
00:10:21,371 --> 00:10:23,873
He multiplies N
by the number of He,

185
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:27,377
and in this room
the thing you'll see."

186
00:10:27,585 --> 00:10:28,837
-Good riddle, huh?
-Yeah.

187
00:10:29,003 --> 00:10:32,757
So when you guys plan fun activities,
does my name even...

188
00:10:32,924 --> 00:10:34,551
come up, or...?

189
00:10:36,094 --> 00:10:38,012
I invited you to my
murder mystery party.

190
00:10:38,179 --> 00:10:43,642
-No, you didn't.
-Oh.

191
00:10:45,103 --> 00:10:47,188
(GROANS) We're the last ones here,
hurry up!

192
00:10:47,355 --> 00:10:50,567
-It is a marathon, not a sprint.
-People run in a marathon!

193
00:10:50,733 --> 00:10:52,569
Not with a bowling ball
on their back.

194
00:10:52,735 --> 00:10:55,071
Hey, Penny, I just
wanted to say good luck,

195
00:10:55,238 --> 00:10:56,614
and I hope there's no hard feelings.

196
00:10:56,781 --> 00:11:00,201
Hey, Romeo! Repair your relationship
on your own time!

197
00:11:01,327 --> 00:11:04,414
Relax, it's a hard puzzle.
It's gonna take a while to solve.

198
00:11:04,581 --> 00:11:06,166
-"Riddle me this--"
-Got it.

199
00:11:07,834 --> 00:11:09,127
Hey, Princeton, look at that,

200
00:11:09,294 --> 00:11:11,379
Team Community College Night School
is in the lead.

201
00:11:13,339 --> 00:11:15,967
I thought we were
the Lightning Sharks!

202
00:11:17,510 --> 00:11:19,554
Hey, you know what this could mean...

203
00:11:21,014 --> 00:11:24,058
Oh, oh, that's good.
Then this would be...

204
00:11:24,225 --> 00:11:26,603
-That's it.
-To the Neil Mobile!

205
00:11:28,521 --> 00:11:30,440
I knew it, we're gonna lose!

206
00:11:30,607 --> 00:11:32,108
Wait, I got it. I got it!

207
00:11:32,275 --> 00:11:34,819
Congratulations, you got it last.

208
00:11:40,909 --> 00:11:43,411
You're really mean, you know that?

209
00:11:50,752 --> 00:11:52,837
So no one's gonna buy anything.

210
00:11:55,798 --> 00:11:58,717
A-ha! Okay...

211
00:11:58,885 --> 00:12:00,345
Let's see.

212
00:12:00,511 --> 00:12:04,057
"To continue on your quest,
leave no stone unturned."

213
00:12:04,224 --> 00:12:06,517
The next clue must be hidden
under one of these rocks.

214
00:12:06,684 --> 00:12:08,603
Okay.

215
00:12:08,770 --> 00:12:09,979
Sheldon, I've got to ask.

216
00:12:10,146 --> 00:12:12,649
How did you figure out
that it was the geology lab?

217
00:12:12,815 --> 00:12:13,608
Oh, simple.

218
00:12:13,775 --> 00:12:16,694
The Arrah Arrah in the riddle
meant Jan Arrah,

219
00:12:16,861 --> 00:12:19,447
a member of the Legion of
Superheroes known as Element Lad.

220
00:12:19,614 --> 00:12:22,784
And then the word "He,"
it wasn't the masculine pronoun,

221
00:12:22,951 --> 00:12:25,536
but rather, "H-E,"
the abbreviation for helium.

222
00:12:25,703 --> 00:12:27,580
See where I'm going with this?

223
00:12:29,666 --> 00:12:32,543
-Yes.
-Nice try.

224
00:12:32,710 --> 00:12:34,879
Now, Element Lad's ability

225
00:12:35,046 --> 00:12:37,048
is the power to transmute
chemical elements.

226
00:12:37,215 --> 00:12:39,634
Helium has an atomic number of two.

227
00:12:39,801 --> 00:12:43,638
If you multiply that by the
atomic number of "N," nitrogen,

228
00:12:43,805 --> 00:12:48,101
you get 14, which is
the atomic number of...?

229
00:12:50,353 --> 00:12:53,731
I'm just funnin' you, silicon.
And that is the most common element

230
00:12:53,898 --> 00:12:55,984
in the Earth's surface.
So that narrowed it down

231
00:12:56,150 --> 00:12:58,069
to the geology lab
or the chemistry lab.

232
00:12:58,236 --> 00:13:00,446
Wow.

233
00:13:00,613 --> 00:13:02,865
I can drink a beer underwater.

234
00:13:04,075 --> 00:13:05,910
And I'm sure your parents are proud.

235
00:13:06,077 --> 00:13:10,415
Now, finally, the line
"in this room the thing you'll see"

236
00:13:10,581 --> 00:13:13,668
was an obvious reference
to Fantastic Four member The Thing ,

237
00:13:13,835 --> 00:13:17,630
-who's made entirely of--
-Shut up. I solved it!

238
00:13:20,383 --> 00:13:21,759
(GASPS)

239
00:13:21,926 --> 00:13:24,095
Those are map coordinates.

240
00:13:24,262 --> 00:13:26,306
Got 'em. Let's go.

241
00:13:26,472 --> 00:13:29,350
Wait. Don't you want to know
how I figured it out?

242
00:13:29,517 --> 00:13:31,144
No one likes a know-it-all, Penny.

243
00:13:35,898 --> 00:13:38,776
(SINGING NEIL DIAMOND'S
"AMERICA")

244
00:13:54,709 --> 00:13:56,044
Um...

245
00:13:56,210 --> 00:14:01,215
I'm okay with you driving my car,
I'm not okay with you flying my car.

246
00:14:01,382 --> 00:14:03,426
Don't sweat it, my dad's a cop.
He can fix things.

247
00:14:05,094 --> 00:14:07,388
Uh-huh. Like death?

248
00:14:07,555 --> 00:14:11,850
Your kid may be an honor student,
but you're a moron!

249
00:14:14,312 --> 00:14:15,938
Penny's not answering my texts.

250
00:14:16,105 --> 00:14:17,815
Who cares?
Focus on the game.

251
00:14:17,982 --> 00:14:20,401
This stupid game is why
she's mad in the first place.

252
00:14:20,568 --> 00:14:22,236
-I'm telling her I'm quitting.
-No!

253
00:14:22,403 --> 00:14:24,822
Quitting would be the worst thing
for your relationship.

254
00:14:24,989 --> 00:14:25,990
Why?

255
00:14:26,157 --> 00:14:29,285
Because it would make you seem like
something she already thinks you are.

256
00:14:29,452 --> 00:14:33,748
-What does she think I am?
-How do I put this?

257
00:14:33,915 --> 00:14:39,087
She's been known to call you a name
that usually applies to a lady part.

258
00:14:41,255 --> 00:14:42,256
Or a cat.

259
00:14:44,258 --> 00:14:46,427
Or a willow.

260
00:14:49,931 --> 00:14:51,766
I can't believe
she would say that about me.

261
00:14:51,933 --> 00:14:55,269
If you're gonna cry about it,
there's tissues in my purse.

262
00:14:55,436 --> 00:14:58,689
Unless you got some in yours.
Big willow.

263
00:15:01,567 --> 00:15:04,529
(SINGING NEIL DIAMOND'S
"AMERICA")

264
00:15:23,714 --> 00:15:27,218
Okay... another 30 feet.

265
00:15:27,385 --> 00:15:29,220
Okay.

266
00:15:29,387 --> 00:15:31,055
Oh. It's a bowling alley.

267
00:15:32,473 --> 00:15:34,600
Yes. Yes!

268
00:15:34,767 --> 00:15:39,730
(YELLING)
My brain is better than everybody's!

269
00:15:43,109 --> 00:15:46,654
(SINGING NEIL DIAMOND'S
"LOVE ON THE ROCKS")

270
00:15:55,329 --> 00:15:58,583
Call me a lady part.
We'll see about that.

271
00:16:01,961 --> 00:16:04,172
-Can you reach the clue?
-Almost.

272
00:16:04,338 --> 00:16:07,633
Hurry, Sheldon and Penny were right
behind us in the bowling alley.

273
00:16:07,800 --> 00:16:10,344
-Got it!
-Great, climb back up.

274
00:16:13,306 --> 00:16:15,057
Come on!

275
00:16:17,310 --> 00:16:19,978
Yeah, Penny might be onto something.

276
00:16:22,273 --> 00:16:24,150
-To the planetarium!
-Let's go!

277
00:16:24,317 --> 00:16:26,527
-To the tar pits!
-Let's go!

278
00:16:26,694 --> 00:16:28,446
There's a Neil Diamond
concert next month!

279
00:16:28,613 --> 00:16:30,323
Let's go!

280
00:16:33,159 --> 00:16:36,245
Three bags, no one's opened 'em.
We're the first ones here!

281
00:16:37,163 --> 00:16:39,916
-It's dirty laundry. You're up.
-Why me?

282
00:16:40,082 --> 00:16:42,502
Because you've been training
for this your whole life.

283
00:16:42,668 --> 00:16:44,879
You live in a pile of dirty laundry.

284
00:16:47,507 --> 00:16:48,841
They're here first.
This is because

285
00:16:49,007 --> 00:16:51,344
you made me slow down
for that blind guy.

286
00:16:51,511 --> 00:16:55,556
-Okay, the clue must be in the bag.
-The clue must be in the bag.

287
00:16:55,723 --> 00:16:58,016
-It's just a bunch of pants.
-It's just a bunch of pants.

288
00:16:59,185 --> 00:17:00,853
You know, I'm surprised
you want to copy my answers

289
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:02,688
since I'm not even smart
enough to be on your team.

290
00:17:02,855 --> 00:17:05,233
Well, why would you want
to be on someone's team

291
00:17:05,399 --> 00:17:08,694
who you like to call a-- I can't
even say it in front of Sheldon.

292
00:17:09,737 --> 00:17:10,947
What are you talking about?

293
00:17:11,113 --> 00:17:12,865
You know exactly
what I'm talking about.

294
00:17:13,031 --> 00:17:15,368
No, she doesn't.
I just made that up.

295
00:17:16,369 --> 00:17:17,662
Why would you do that?

296
00:17:17,828 --> 00:17:20,373
Because you were about to quit like
a big-- Sheldon, cover your ears.

297
00:17:22,083 --> 00:17:25,586
I'm not a child.
I know the word "ninny."

298
00:17:26,212 --> 00:17:28,548
Yeah, well, you should have quit
'cause I'm still gonna beat your ass.

299
00:17:28,714 --> 00:17:31,467
Wish I had a man
like her on my team.

300
00:17:31,634 --> 00:17:34,804
Hey, I am every bit
as much of a man as Penny.

301
00:17:34,971 --> 00:17:37,014
Now, let's do this!

302
00:17:37,181 --> 00:17:39,934
Wait, it's not all pants,
there's one shirt.

303
00:17:40,101 --> 00:17:42,687
-Oh.
-Hey, that's my shirt.

304
00:17:42,853 --> 00:17:44,897
-This one is, too.
-No, no, that's not mine.

305
00:17:45,063 --> 00:17:47,441
-That has a big spot on it.
-Wait. So does this one.

306
00:17:47,608 --> 00:17:49,360
Maybe the spot's the clue.

307
00:17:54,740 --> 00:17:57,410
"Sheldon's spot."
The coin is in your spot!

308
00:17:58,911 --> 00:18:01,289
-Oh, that's clever.
-PENNY: Hurry!

309
00:18:01,455 --> 00:18:04,250
Be there in a minute.
I just have to pre-soak these!

310
00:18:08,212 --> 00:18:10,590
(ALL YELLING)

311
00:18:10,798 --> 00:18:14,010
Stop her, Leonard! Stop her!

312
00:18:17,763 --> 00:18:19,724
(ALL YELLING)

313
00:18:20,766 --> 00:18:24,061
-Where the hell's the coin?
-Wasn't the answer Sheldon's spot?

314
00:18:24,228 --> 00:18:28,399
Oh, yes, Leonard. Yes, it was.

315
00:18:29,400 --> 00:18:32,570
-Then where's the coin?
-Yes, exactly.

316
00:18:32,737 --> 00:18:34,113
Where is the coin?

317
00:18:35,531 --> 00:18:37,825
Why don't you look in your pockets?

318
00:18:41,787 --> 00:18:44,415
I slipped them in there
earlier today.

319
00:18:46,459 --> 00:18:49,754
-I don't get it.
-Don't you see?

320
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:54,008
When we're all having fun together,
we're already winners.

321
00:18:54,175 --> 00:18:55,968
Oh, look. See?

322
00:18:56,135 --> 00:18:58,262
Even I'm a winner.

323
00:19:00,473 --> 00:19:01,724
Are you kidding me?

324
00:19:03,142 --> 00:19:05,728
That is the stupidest thing
I've ever heard.

325
00:19:06,937 --> 00:19:10,816
You suck so hard.

326
00:19:12,109 --> 00:19:14,987
Well, I thought...
Come on, I didn't want anyone

327
00:19:15,154 --> 00:19:16,947
to feel bad at the end of the game.

328
00:19:17,114 --> 00:19:19,241
And some of those puzzles
were really hard

329
00:19:19,408 --> 00:19:20,910
and I didn't know
who was gonna get Penny.

330
00:19:27,291 --> 00:19:29,001
Run.

331
00:19:29,168 --> 00:19:32,463
Okay, okay, that came out wrong,
but you have to admit,

332
00:19:32,630 --> 00:19:34,715
you all had a wonderful time.

333
00:19:36,050 --> 00:19:38,219
Run to India.

334
00:19:39,095 --> 00:19:42,473
I just wanted to do
something beautiful!

335
00:19:46,352 --> 00:19:49,187
Hey, look. I won.

336
00:19:56,362 --> 00:19:59,490
(SINGING NEIL DIAMOND'S
"CHERRY, CHERRY")

